3b:["$","$L4c",null,{"type":"multiple","children":[["$","$L4d",null,{"title":"Why did two identical builds not share work?","id":"why-no-merge","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Check the three fields in the merge key first: instance name, digest\nfunction, action digest. A different instance name is the usual answer,\nand the second most common is one client sending ",["$","code",null,{"children":"skip_cache_lookup"}],",\nwhich opts that action out of merging entirely."]}]}],["$","$L4d",null,{"title":"My queue depth is zero but clients are waiting. Why?","id":"queue-depth-zero","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Cache hits never enter the queue; on a Bazel client they are answered by\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"GetActionResult"}]," before ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Execute"}]," is ever sent, and with a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"cache_lookup"}],"\nscheduler they are answered by the decorator in front of the queue. If\nclients are genuinely waiting with an empty queue, look at whether the\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"Execute"}]," calls are being rejected up front (missing inputs return\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"FAILED_PRECONDITION"}],") rather than queued."]}]}],["$","$L4d",null,{"title":"What does the Redis backend change?","id":"redis-backend-differences","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["It swaps the state layer for one backed by RediSearch indexes with\noptimistic concurrency. Two consequences: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Code::Aborted"}]," from an internal\noperation means \"reload and retry\", not \"failed\"; it is the normal\noutcome of a version conflict. And action merging becomes best-effort,\nbecause there is a small window where a just-written action is not yet\nvisible to the index; a short retry narrows it but does not close it. Two\nidentical actions submitted within milliseconds of each other may both\nrun."]}]}],["$","$L4d",null,{"title":"Is fallback_match_interval_s just a safety net?","id":"fallback-match-interval","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Not with the Redis backend. Matching there is driven by pub-sub with this\ninterval as a poll fallback, and pub-sub delivery is not guaranteed, so\nthe interval is load-bearing, and raising it raises worst-case dispatch\nlatency directly. It defaults to five seconds."}]}],["$","$L4d",null,{"title":"Can I stop one team starving another?","id":"priority-starvation","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Not inside the scheduler. Ordering is priority-dominant with no aging and\nno per-client fairness, so a client submitting everything at high priority\nwill starve everyone below it. Enforcement has to sit in front of the\nscheduler: separate instance names with separate schedulers, or an\nadmission layer that normalises priority."}]}]]}]
